# Framework parity / porting

Use with `framework-parity-porting`. This rule holds
invariants. The skill holds the playbook. Do not duplicate isolation,
substrate, hygiene, GUI, or verification checklists here — call those skills.

## When this rule applies

Only when the operator **both**:

1. Points at foreign application code (repo URL, clone path, directory, or
   attached tree), and
2. Asks to assess, plan, port, or achieve parity into this framework.

Ignore this rule for explain-only reviews and ordinary in-repo work.

## Invariants

1. **Orchestrate, do not duplicate.** Call `framework-addon-isolation`,
   `framework-addon-substrate`, `framework-addon-hygiene-checks`,
   `framework-addon-modularity`, `code-deslop`, `code-refactor`,
   `framework-gui-appearance` / page patterns, `code-workflow`,
   `code-verification-before-completion`, and `browser-automation` as
   needed. Do not paste their checklists into new assets.

2. **Phase gate.** Assess → Plan → operator go → Port → Verify. First-turn
   `port` / `parity` language is trigger only — it does not authorize
   implementation. After the Plan Jira comment exists, stop until proceed
   language (`go`, `proceed`, `implement`, post-Plan `port it`, or same-turn
   execute with Plan already present). No addon dirs, remotes, or port commits
   during Assess/Plan unless that exception applies.

3. **Foreign source is read-only** unless the operator explicitly asks to edit
   it. Implementation lives under `addons/<name>/` (rare substrate under
   `framework/addons/`).

4. **Port shapes.** Support `full-app` and `feature-island`. Assess must record
   which shape applies and scope the matrix to it.

5. **Parity matrix is the plan.** Publish on the work Jira issue before port
   coding. Required columns: source capability; parity rank
   (`capability` / `behavior-API` / `GUI-flow`); target
   (`addon` / `substrate` / `drop` / `defer`); owning addon when target is
   addon; GUI strategy (`base` / `addon-variant` + reason); verify method
   (`test` / `browser` / `both`); verify note (short how-to-verify /
   browser checklist — required for `browser` / `both`). Include do-not-port
   map and proposed addon names in the same comment. Screenshots optional;
   pixel-perfect UI is not AC.

6. **Parity rank.** Prove capability first, then behavior/API, then GUI-flow.
   Visual look is not acceptance unless the operator raises it for a row.
   Pixel-perfect clone is not required by default.

7. **GUI.** Prefer base components and framework page patterns. Addon-local
   variants only for clearly unreplicable special behavior named in the
   matrix.

8. **Do-not-port map.** Map source auth, Docker, CI, config, routing, and
   storage to framework equivalents. Port behavior. Do not copy their
   substrate.

9. **Pattern conflict.** Default: adapt to framework norms. Clearly better and
   reusable → propose substrate (separate framework Story when non-trivial).
   Product-specific excellence → keep in the addon. Record the choice on the
   matrix row.

10. **Addon names.** Operator confirms proposed name(s) before creating
    `addons/<name>/` or remotes when a new name or split is proposed. One
    source app may become N addons.

11. **Vertical slices.** One user journey end-to-end (API + GUI + tests)
    before the next.

12. **Hard refuse.** Secrets and production data copy; product identity or
    purpose in tracked framework; non-trivial substrate smuggled into the
    product port; full port from explain-only language.

13. **Verification.** Every ported matrix row has a verify method. Tests live
    under the owning addon. Run isolation + substrate hygiene before claiming
    a slice done. Browser dogfood when the matrix requires it.

14. **Isolation.** Tracked framework stays stranger-safe. Use invented
    vocabulary only in tracked policy examples (`addons/<name>/`, `example`,
    `alpha` / `beta`).
